<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Everything Xcode can derive from build settings stays in the build
	     settings; GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE merges those keys on top of this
	     file. So this file holds only the keys that have no INFOPLIST_KEY_
	     counterpart. -->

	<!-- The single most effective power setting Retain has, and the reason it
	     is already here in a build that records nothing yet: it is a bundle
	     fact, not a code path, so putting it in now means no later phase can
	     forget it.

	     Core Audio reads this hint when it hands the process an input stream
	     and sizes the IO buffer accordingly — 4096 frames instead of the 512
	     it would otherwise pick. At 16 kHz that is a wakeup every 256 ms
	     rather than every 32 ms: eight times fewer trips out of idle for the
	     same audio. Retain is meant to run for a whole school day on battery,
	     and this costs nothing but latency Retain does not need, because
	     nothing here is monitored live through the speakers.

	     Verify it survived into the bundle rather than assuming it:
	         plutil -p "$BUILT_PRODUCTS_DIR/Retain.app/Contents/Info.plist" \
	           | grep AudioHardwarePowerHint -->
	<key>AudioHardwarePowerHint</key>
	<string>Favor Saving Power</string>
	<!-- macOS shows this string verbatim in the permission sheet, so it says
	     where the audio goes rather than restating that the app wants a
	     microphone. Retain's whole claim is that the recording never leaves the
	     machine, and the permission sheet is the one moment a user actually
	     asks that question, so it is answered there rather than in a settings
	     pane they may never open. -->
	<!-- DM Sans is the one family the design draws in, and it is not on macOS,
	     so it ships in the bundle and is registered from there. This is the
	     macOS key; `UIAppFonts` is the iOS one and does nothing here.

	     The value is "." and not "Fonts" on purpose. `Retain/Resources/Fonts/`
	     is a folder inside a synchronized group, and Xcode copies the files it
	     contains flat into `Contents/Resources` rather than keeping the folder,
	     so the directory the fonts actually end up in is the Resources
	     directory itself — which is what "." names. Preserving the folder would
	     need a folder reference in `project.pbxproj`, and that file is not
	     touched for resources.

	     A font that fails to register does not fail loudly: text falls back to
	     the system face and every measurement in the design is quietly wrong.
	     `RetainTypography.isDesignFontAvailable` checks it at runtime and a test
	     asserts it, so the failure has somewhere to show up. -->
	<key>ATSApplicationFontsPath</key>
	<string>.</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Retain records the lecture through your microphone. The audio, the transcript and the notes stay on this Mac — nothing is uploaded, and only the lectures you keep are stored.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
